{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>And he said: Cursed be Canaan.</b> Concerning him it is said: ומכרתי את בניכם ואת בנותיכם." "<b>Cursed be Canaan.</b> Ham sinned and Canaan was cursed? Rather, since it is written: ויברך אלקים את נח ואת בניו, and there is no curse in the place of blessing, therefore: and he said, Cursed be Canaan. And another interpretation: Ham did nothing to him but saw him exposed naked and did not cover him as Shem and Japheth did, and he went and revealed the matter outside, and did not intend that they should cover him — for if he wished, he could have covered him. Therefore he was not blessed. And Canaan his son heard and did to Noah his grandfather what he did, therefore Noah cursed him. And a proof for the matter: אשר עשה לו בנו הקטן — in the sons of Ham, and one cannot say the youngest of the sons of Noah, for Ham was not the youngest among the sons, but rather Japheth was the eldest, Ham the second, Shem the third, as is proven below. And Shem and Japheth, who busied themselves with the mitzvah, he blessed them; and Ham, who did not honor him, he neither blessed nor cursed; and Canaan, who despised him, he cursed." "<b>Cursed be Canaan.</b> He cursed him according to his name. He said to him: What is your name? Canaan. Your name causes that you shall be נכנע (subdued) to your brothers like a slave, who nods before his master, and when he does not do his master's will, his master curses him with the language of ארור." "<b>A slave of slaves shall he be.</b> Not by means of war, but by means of war — כל נשמה shall not live."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:03:46.571491579Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}